     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 4000 mg four times daily comes to about 228 mg/kg/day for a 70 kg patient. That's in the range where ibuprofen overdose toxicity is expected: GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS effects. I don't know of any indication where that dose helps, and the ceiling doesn't go up with normal renal and hepatic function. Once it's in the system, nursing could give it as written.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, 800 mg PO three times daily or 600 mg PO four times daily, with food. Both are 2,400 mg/day.\n- **Add a note to the order,** such as a GI prophylaxis plan or renal monitoring, if you want one.\n- **Look at other options** if the pain or inflammation isn't controlled at the maximum dose. Scheduled acetaminophen, a different analgesic strategy, or a pharmacy or pain-service consult could all help.\n\nIf I've misread the situation, for example if the order is meant for something other than ibuprofen or the units are different, tell me and I'll re-check. If you need to go above the standard limits, please do it through pharmacy and your institution's override process."
      }
     ],
